CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the slicing method for computing the volume of a solid of known cross-sectional area. Left panel: a solid S extending along the x-axis from x = a to x = b, divided into n slabs S1, S2, ..., Sn by partition points a = x0, x1, ..., x_{n-1}, b = xn marked along the base axis; one slab is highlighted in blue. Right panel (zoom): the kth slab Sk is isolated and shown as a right prism with width Delta_xk along the x-axis; a caption reads The cross section here has area A(x*_k). An arrow connects the two views. The figure sets up the Riemann sum sum A(x*_k) Delta_xk as an approximation for the total volume, whose limit gives the definite integral V = integral from a to b of A(x) dx.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Solid S: a loaf-shaped prism lying along the x-axis from x=a (left) to x=b (right), with vertical rectangular cross sections perpendicular to x whose height h(x) grows from left to right along a gently curved top surface
  - Pale pink translucent faces of S with red outline edges (front face, top curved surface, right end cap, bottom rectangle)
  - Partition cut lines drawn across the top and front of S at x1, x2, ..., x_{n-1}, dividing S into slabs S1, S2, ..., Sn
  - One highlighted slab (default the 3rd) filled solid blue instead of pink
  - Horizontal x-axis line below the solid with right-pointing arrowhead
  - Tick marks and labels on the axis: a, x1, x2, ..., (ellipsis), x_{n-1}, b
  - Slab labels S1, S2 on front faces near the left, Sn on the right-most slab, S on the top surface
  - Curved tan arrow sweeping from the highlighted slab in the left panel to the right (zoom) panel
  - Zoom panel: the kth slab Sk drawn as a thin blue translucent right prism standing on the x-axis
  - Zoom panel: gray wireframe outlines of the neighboring slab shells behind/around Sk
  - Zoom panel: thick black vertical line marking the cross section at x = x*_k on the front face of Sk
  - Zoom panel: black dot on the x-axis at x*_k with label x*_k beneath
  - Zoom panel: horizontal x-axis with arrowhead and 'x' label
  - Zoom panel: Δx_k dimension line with inward-pointing arrows under the slab width
  - Zoom panel: label Sk on the right face of the slab
  - Tan rounded callout box with the text 'The cross section here has area A(x*_k).' and a thick tan arrow pointing at the highlighted cross section
  - Readout of the Riemann sum Σ A(x*_k) Δx_k and the exact volume ∫_a^b A(x) dx

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World units: x runs a = -3 to b = 3 (length 6, screen-right = +X). Depth of the solid along the receding axis +Z is d = 1.6, constant. Height along +Y is h(x) = 0.7 + 1.5*sqrt((x - a)/(b - a)) — so h(a) ≈ 0.7 rising with a concave-down curve to h(b) ≈ 2.2, matching the top surface that climbs left-to-right and flattens near the right end; the right end is a flat vertical cap. Cross-sectional area A(x) = d * h(x) = 1.6*h(x); slab volume = A(x*_k)*Δx_k with Δx_k = 6/n and x*_k the midpoint of the kth subinterval. Exact volume V = ∫_{-3}^{3} 1.6*h(x) dx ≈ 12.3 cubic units. The solid sits with its bottom face on y = 0; the x-axis line is drawn at y = -0.15 slightly in front (larger -Z) of the solid and extends from x = -4 to x = 4 with an arrowhead at the right. Tick labels a, x1, x2, …, x_{n-1}, b sit just below the axis at the cut positions. Colors: solid body pale pink fill #F7CFCF at ~0.55 opacity with red edges #E23B3B (line width ~2); highlighted slab fill medium blue #7FB3DC at ~0.7 opacity with darker blue edge #3C7CB4; partition cut lines thin black #1A1A1A; axis and text black #000000; callout box fill #FAE3B0 with border #D8A63F and black text; sweeping connector arrow gradient tan #F1DCAA with gray outline #B9B0A0; neighbour-slab wireframe in zoom panel gray #9A9A9A. Zoom panel: same slab geometry scaled up ~2.2x, placed to the right of the solid at about x = +7 world units, its thickness Δx_k drawn along +X, its height along +Y and depth along +Z; a thick black vertical line (width ~3) marks the cross section on the near-left face at x*_k, with a filled black dot on the axis directly below and the Δx_k dimension line beneath that, arrows pointing inward toward the slab edges. Label Sk on the right-hand face of the zoomed slab, labels S1, S2 on the front faces of the first two slabs of the solid, Sn on the last, and S on the top surface near x = +0.5.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitSolid",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The slabs are genuine 3D prisms stacked along x; rotating shows that each slice has a two-dimensional cross-sectional face whose area, not its outline, determines the slab's volume."
  },
  {
    "id": "slabCount",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Number of slabs n",
    "range": [
      1,
      40,
      1
    ],
    "default": 6,
    "teaches": "As n grows and each Δx_k shrinks, the stack of flat-topped prisms hugs the curved solid ever more closely and the Riemann sum Σ A(x*_k)Δx_k converges to the true volume ∫_a^b A(x) dx."
  },
  {
    "id": "selectSlab",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "Dragging the zoom selection along the x-axis shows that A(x*_k) changes with position while Δx_k stays fixed — the cross-sectional area is a function of x, so each slab contributes a different amount of volume."
  },
  {
    "id": "panelView",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "Solid S",
      "Zoom on slab S_k",
      "Side by side"
    ],
    "default": "Solid S",
    "teaches": "Moving from the whole partitioned solid to the isolated slab makes explicit that the zoom panel is one term A(x*_k)Δx_k of the sum drawn from the left-hand figure."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The solid S",
    "narration": "The solid S lies along the x-axis from x = a to x = b. Every plane perpendicular to the axis cuts it in a cross section whose area A(x) varies with x.",
    "state": {
      "panelView": "Solid S",
      "slabCount": 1
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Partition into slabs",
    "narration": "Cutting at x1, x2, …, x_{n-1} splits S into slabs S1, S2, …, Sn. Each slab is approximated by a prism of constant cross section.",
    "state": {
      "panelView": "Solid S",
      "slabCount": 6
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Zoom on the kth slab",
    "narration": "Isolating the highlighted slab: its face at x*_k has area A(x*_k) and its thickness is Δx_k, so its volume is approximately A(x*_k)Δx_k.",
    "state": {
      "panelView": "Zoom on slab S_k",
      "slabCount": 6
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Adding the slabs up",
    "narration": "Side by side, the zoomed slab is one term of the sum Σ A(x*_k)Δx_k shown beside the whole solid.",
    "state": {
      "panelView": "Side by side",
      "slabCount": 6
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Refining the partition",
    "narration": "With n = 30 the slabs become thin wafers, the flat steps vanish, and the sum settles on V = ∫_a^b A(x) dx.",
    "state": {
      "panelView": "Side by side",
      "slabCount": 30
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.62
}

NOTES: Build the solid as a single BufferGeometry lofted from vertical rectangular cross sections sampled along x (x, ±d/2, 0..h(x)) so the top surface is smooth; rebuild slab meshes on slabCount change by extruding each subinterval as a box of constant height h(x*_k) (flat-topped prism) shown at ~0.55 opacity over a faint ghost of the true smooth solid, so the stair-step error is visible and shrinks with n. Use MeshBasicMaterial/MeshLambertMaterial with transparent:true, depthWrite:false, and render back-to-front by sorting slabs along the view direction to avoid transparency artifacts. Draw all red/black outlines with LineSegments (EdgesGeometry) rather than wireframe. Labels (a, x1, …, S1, Sn, Sk, x*_k, Δx_k) as CSS2DObject/HTML overlays so they stay upright while orbiting; hide intermediate partition labels and replace with '…' once n > 8. The tan callout box, sweeping arrow, Δx_k dimension line and the Riemann-sum readout are HTML/SVG overlay elements anchored to projected 3D points. Toggle 'Side by side' by translating the zoom group into frame and shrinking the main solid group ~0.7x rather than rendering two cameras.

